u are heading into government with a Conservative majority even narrower than David Cameron’s 2015 victory. Now you have to think about getting back to your job as Prime Minister. In your next term, there’s a lot on your plate. Brexit is the biggest elephant in the room, the country is demanding that your government can handle this as effectively as possible, as you’re scheduled to sit down in many meeting with Donald Tusk in Brussels, but in the wake of that, you face challenges from the fringes of the party, intensely questioning your strength as Prime Minister, possibly looking for any opportunity to replace you for a leader more amicable to their Brexit goals., It’s very unlikely that the party will be confident in you as Prime Minister especially as you lost seats compared to Cameron. There is a chance that you might still be able to deliver Brexit, but it’s very unlikely you’ll see the next General election scheduled no later than 2022, based on your rather poor showing in an election you should have won by many more seats than you did.</p>"]
